items.content:
@ShahabBakht I feel like the progress of AI is actually making systems neuroscience more exciting. 

It's not like current AI has a particularly good angle on physical intuition, common sense, world models, planning/reasoning, motivations/drives, emotions, etc. 

We may now have the tools to implement and test cognitive architectures.

@iamharikrishna1 @ShahabBakht If it can plan, it can predict the consequences of its actions and envisage multiple outcomes, then pick a sequence of actions that accomplishes the goal.
That means it has a world model that can make real-world prediction.
That means it's not an LLM.

@yoavgo He was not a professor until  2002.
So, that's 16 PhD students in 24 years, which is one graduating student every 18 months on average. 

He was a research scientist at GTE, U Mass, and AT&amp;T Labs-Research (where we were colleagues) before 2002.

@KettlebellDan forget politics. his opposition to Lidar is stupid, as I have written multiple times.

also his estimates on driverless cars were off by at least a decade. 

etc. i don’t think he is actually stupid but his inability to listen to others that disagree with him is in fact stupid.

@davisrules1994 @ShahabBakht Yes, emotions are necessary.
Emotions are the effect of intrinsic motivations or the anticipation of outcome.
Both of those things are necessary for intelligent behavior.

